`]}),e.jsx(u,{type:"key",children:e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Check length as well as accuracy"})}),e.jsxs(n,{title:"Three routes to 4 bits, and a verbosity check",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Plain quantization-aware training (QAT) trains with fake quantization in the loop. Three other routes target similar 4-bit formats. Nemotron 3 Nano uses NVFP4 quantization-aware distillation ",e.jsx(i,{id:"nvidia2025nemotron3nano",children:"NVIDIA, 2025b"}),". Nemotron 3 Super uses native NVFP4 pretraining, so it trains in the low-precision format from the start ",e.jsx(i,{id:"nvidia2026nemotron3super",children:"NVIDIA, 2026a"}),". gpt-oss uses quantization-aware post-training for its MXFP4 MoE weights ",e.jsx(i,{id:"openai2025gptoss",children:"OpenAI, 2025"}),". These routes differ from plain QAT, even though they target similar deployment formats."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Nemotron 3.5 Super shows what to check after quantizing. A sensitivity analysis of its NVFP4 recipe found that the output layer, ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"lm_head"}),", accounts for nearly half of the NVFP4 sensitivity ",e.jsx(i,{id:"nvidia2026nemotron35super",children:"NVIDIA, 2026b"}),". In the report's ablations, quantization moved generation length more than accuracy. FP4 weights in the ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"lm_head"})," leave accuracy unchanged but shift the margin between stopping and continuing, so the model's answers change length. The release criteria require at least 99% median accuracy recovery and at most a 5% increase in verbosity over the BF16 model ",e.jsx(i,{id:"nvidia2026nemotron35super",children:"NVIDIA, 2026b"}),"."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Accuracy recovery alone does not validate a quantized model, and output length needs its own check. Measuring both on a quantized course model would make a good final project."})]})]})]}),`
`,e.jsxs(l,{tab:"Summary",plate:"leupold-tab-xxix",children:[e.jsxs(s,{title:"Every technique pays somewhere",accent:1,number:"42",items:"4",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Linear attention: recall traded for a fixed-size state"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Hybrids: a few attention layers, memory grows slowly"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"LoRA: adapter capacity for optimizer memory, merged free"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Ask what changes, where you pay, where it pays off"}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(n,{title:"Every technique pays somewhere",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The opening question was how to judge a new technique before you spend your budget on it. Ask three questions. What changes, on which of the five axes and in which setting? Where do you pay? Where does it pay off?"}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The two topics taught in depth show the pattern. Linear attention replaces the growing score matrix and KV cache with a fixed-size recurrent state, so its sequence cost is ",e.jsx(a,{math:"O(L)",display:!1})," and its state does not grow with the context. It pays with recall. A state of fixed size compresses the whole history and cannot look up an arbitrary old token. Gated DeltaNet makes the forgetting selective: its gate erases old content, and the delta rule rewrites the value stored under a key. Hybrids buy recall back with a few full-attention layers. Those layers keep a KV cache, so the hybrid's memory still grows with the context, only more slowly."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"LoRA freezes the base model and trains a low-rank update. It pays with adapter capacity and saves most of the gradient and optimizer memory of full fine-tuning. In the right setting it reaches about the quality of full fine-tuning, and once merged it costs nothing at inference."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The other five techniques pay elsewhere. Hyper-connections expand the residual into several streams, and their reported quality gains spend added FLOPs and memory traffic. Engram adds conditional memory, a sparsity axis complementary to the mixture of experts. Its quality gains are bound to the paper's 27B comparison at equal parameters and FLOPs ",e.jsx(i,{id:"cheng2026engram",children:"Cheng et al., 2026"}),". MTP adds future-token predictors and auxiliary losses during training. Its reported quality gains depend on the method and the setting ",e.jsx(i,{id:"gloeckle2024mtp",children:"Gloeckle et al., 2024"}),", and retained predictors can supply the drafter of Week 10 ",e.jsx(i,{id:"deepseekai2024deepseekv3",children:"DeepSeek-AI, 2024"}),". FiM adds infilling by reordering documents, at a small training cost and with no change to the architecture. Quantization stores fewer bits per weight for less memory, with latency gains only on matching hardware and kernels."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Every technique on the map pays somewhere: find where before you spend your budget. Each of the seven can seed a final project, and the three questions are also how you scope one. Name the axis you expect to move, the setting where you pay, and the measurement that would show the payoff."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Week 9 asks the same question of the training data: what each filter costs, and what it buys."})]})]}),e.jsx(s,{title:"To-dos until next week",accent:2,number:"43",children:e.jsxs("div",{className:"flex flex-col gap-10",children:[e.jsx("div",{className:"flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-x-24 gap-y-6",children:e.jsxs("figure",{className:"text-center",children:[e.jsx("a",{href:"https://tally.so/r/zEMNDE",children:e.jsx("img",{src:"images/qr-week-08-muddiest-point.svg",alt:"QR code for the muddiest-point survey, tally.so/r/zEMNDE",className:"w-56 h-56"})}),e.jsxs("figcaption",{children:[e.jsx("strong",{children:"Muddiest point"}),e.jsx("br",{}),"Do it now (2 min)"]})]})}),e.jsxs("div",{className:"grid gap-x-12 gap-y-6 md:grid-cols-3",children:[e.jsxs("div",{children:[e.jsx("div",{className:"font-semibold",children:"Thursday 10 December: no lecture"}),e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
